    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor acts as an open circuit once it is fully charged, blocking any further current flow after the transient phase is complete. In contrast, an AC circuit causes the capacitor to continuously charge and discharge as the voltage alternates, allowing current to effectively pass through the component. The opposition to this AC current, known as capacitive reactance, is calculated using the formula $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}$.",
